### Response:
Here's a Java method that checks if a nested integer contains an even number, without using any built-in functions or libraries for arithmetic operations or checks:

```java
public class NestedInteger {
    int value;
    NestedInteger next;
    NestedInteger child;

    public NestedInteger(int value) {
        this.value = value;
    }

    public NestedInteger(int value, NestedInteger next) {
        this.value = value;
        this.next = next;
    }

    public NestedInteger(int value, NestedInteger next, NestedInteger child) {
        this.value = value;
        this.next = next;
        this.child = child;
    }
}

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Create a sample nested integer structure
        NestedInteger nestedInteger = new NestedInteger(1,
                new NestedInteger(2, null,
                        new NestedInteger(3,
                                new NestedInteger(4,
                                        new NestedInteger(5,
                                                new NestedInteger(6,
                                                        new NestedInteger(7, null,
                                                                new NestedInteger(8))))))),
                new NestedInteger(9));

        boolean containsEven = containsEven(nestedInteger);
        System.out.println(containsEven); // Output: true
    }

    public static boolean containsEven(NestedInteger nestedInteger) {
        if (nestedInteger == null) {
            return false;
        }

        if (nestedInteger.value % 2 == 0) {
            return true;
        }

        // Check the child recursively
        boolean containsEvenChild = containsEven(nestedInteger.child);

        // Check the next nested integer recursively
        boolean containsEvenNext = containsEven(nestedInteger.next);

        return containsEvenChild || containsEvenNext;
    }
}
```

Explanation:
1. We define a class `NestedInteger` that represents a nested integer. It has three properties: `value` represents the value of the nested integer, `next` represents the next nested integer at the same level, and `child` represents the first nested integer at the next level.
2. In the `containsEven` method, we first check if the current nested integer's value is even. If it is, we return `true`.
3. Then, we recursively check if the child nested integer or the next nested integer (at the same level) contains an even number. We use the logical OR operator (`||`) to combine the results.
4. The base case for the recursion is when the nested integer is `null`, in which case we return `false` to indicate that there is no even number found.

The time complexity of this method is O(n), where n is the total number of integers in the nested structure. This is because we traverse all the nested integers exactly once. The space complexity is O(1) because we don't use any additional data structures to keep track of the nested structure.

