The original test was asking to print out the Fibonacci sequence up to 1 million, which can be easily achieved by using a loop. However, increasing the limit to 1 billion requires a more efficient approach, as the previous method would be too slow and resource-intensive.

To solve this problem, we can use the concept of memoization. Memoization is a technique where we store the values of previously computed Fibonacci numbers to avoid redundant calculations.

Here's the modified code to solve the problem:

```java
public class FibonacciSequence {
    private static Map<Integer, BigInteger> memo = new HashMap<>();

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int limit = 1000000000;

        for (int i = 1; i <= limit; i++) {
            System.out.println(fibonacci(i));
        }
    }

    private static BigInteger fibonacci(int n) {
        if (n <= 2) {
            return BigInteger.ONE;
        }

        if (memo.containsKey(n)) {
            return memo.get(n);
        }

        BigInteger fib = fibonacci(n - 1).add(fibonacci(n - 2));
        memo.put(n, fib);

        return fib;
    }
}
```

In this code, we introduce a memo map to store previously computed Fibonacci numbers. When calculating the nth Fibonacci number, we first check if it exists in the memo map. If it does, we simply return the stored value. Otherwise, we calculate the Fibonacci number using the recursive formula and store it in the memo map for future reference.

By using memoization, we can significantly reduce the computation time and solve the problem efficiently even for larger limits like 1 billion.

