First, we handle edge cases by throwing an exception for negative indices. For indices 0 and 1, the Fibonacci number is equal to the index itself.

We use a memoization technique to store previously computed Fibonacci numbers in a map called `memo`. Before computing the Fibonacci number at a given index, we check if it has already been computed and stored in the `memo` map. If it is, we simply return the stored value. Otherwise, we compute it recursively using the formula `fibonacci(index - 1) + fibonacci(index - 2)`. We then store the computed value in the `memo` map before returning it.

In the `main` method, we test the `fibonacci` method by passing an index of 10 and printing the result.

This code has a time complexity of O(n) due to the use of memoization. Without memoization, the time complexity would be O(2^n).

