Here's an example solution to check if a given number is a palindrome number using recursion and without converting the number to a string:

```javascript
function isPalindromeNumber(num) {
  // Base cases
  if (num < 0) {
    return false; // Negative numbers are not palindromic
  }
  if (num >= 0 && num < 10) {
    return true; // Single digit numbers are palindromic
  }

  // Recursive case
  const divisor = Math.pow(10, Math.floor(Math.log10(num))); // Get the divisor to extract the first digit
  const firstDigit = Math.floor(num / divisor);
  const lastDigit = num % 10;

  if (firstDigit !== lastDigit) {
    return false; // If first and last digits are not equal, it's not a palindrome
  }

  // Recursively check the number without the first and last digits
  const remainingNum = Math.floor((num % divisor) / 10);
  return isPalindromeNumber(remainingNum);
}

// Example usage
console.log(isPalindromeNumber(12321)); // Output: true
console.log(isPalindromeNumber(12345)); // Output: false
console.log(isPalindromeNumber(123321)); // Output: true
console.log(isPalindromeNumber(-12321)); // Output: false
```

This solution recursively checks the first and last digits of the number. If they are equal, it recursively calls itself with the remaining number without the first and last digits. If any pair of first and last digits are not equal, it returns false. The base cases handle single-digit numbers and negative numbers.

