```java
import java.util.HashMap;
import java.util.Map;

public class Factorial {
    private static Map<Integer, Long> memo = new HashMap<>();

    public static long factorial(int n) {
        if (n < 0) {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("Factorial is undefined for negative numbers");
        }

        if (n == 0) {
            return 1;
        }

        if (memo.containsKey(n)) {
            return memo.get(n);
        }

        long result = multiply(n, factorial(n - 1));
        memo.put(n, result);
        return result;
    }

    private static long multiply(int a, long b) {
        if (a == 0 || b == 0) {
            return 0;
        }

        if (a == 1) {
            return b;
        }

        return b + multiply(a - 1, b);
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Test cases
        int[] numbers = {0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 10, 20, 30};
        for (int num : numbers) {
            try {
                long result = factorial(num);
                System.out.println("Factorial of " + num + " is: " + result);
            } catch (IllegalArgumentException e) {
                System.out.println(e.getMessage());
            }
        }
    }
}
```

The `factorial()` method checks for negative numbers and zero, throwing an `IllegalArgumentException` with an appropriate error message. It also utilizes memoization to avoid redundant calculations, storing previously computed factorials in a map.

The `multiply()` method recursively multiplies two numbers by repeatedly adding the second number `b` to itself `a` times. It handles the base cases when either number is zero or when `a` is 1.

