```java
public class SumOfSquares {
    public static int sumOfSquares(int[] arr, int index) {
        // Base case: if index reaches the end of the array, return 0
        if (index == arr.length) {
            return 0;
        }

        int num = arr[index];

        // If the number is negative, square the absolute value
        if (num < 0) {
            num = Math.abs(num);
        }

        // Recursive call to sumOfSquares function for the next index
        int sum = num * num + sumOfSquares(arr, index + 1);

        return sum;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] arr = {4, -2, 6, 8};
        int sum = sumOfSquares(arr, 0);
        System.out.println(sum); // Output: 120
    }
}
```

In this code, the `sumOfSquares` function takes two parameters: the integer array `arr` and the current `index` being processed. 

The base case is when the `index` reaches the end of the array (`index == arr.length`), in which case we return 0. 

For each number in the array, we first check if it is negative. If it is, we square the absolute value using `Math.abs` function. 

We then recursively call the `sumOfSquares` function for the next index (`index + 1`), and add the square of the current number to the result of the recursive call. 

