### Response:
Here's a Java program that checks if an input number is an Armstrong number and prints all the Armstrong numbers within a given range using recursion:

```java
public class ArmstrongNumbersRecursion {

    // Recursive function to calculate the power of a number
    public static int power(int num, int p) {
        if (p == 0)
            return 1;
        return num * power(num, p - 1);
    }

    // Recursive function to check if a number is an Armstrong number
    public static boolean isArmstrong(int num, int p) {
        if (num == 0)
            return true;
        int digit = num % 10;
        return power(digit, p) + isArmstrong(num / 10, p - 1);
    }

    // Recursive function to print all Armstrong numbers within a given range
    public static void printArmstrongNumbers(int start, int end, int p) {
        if (start > end)
            return;
        if (isArmstrong(start, p))
            System.out.println(start);
        printArmstrongNumbers(start + 1, end, p);
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int start = 1;
        int end = 1000;
        int p = String.valueOf(end).length();

        System.out.println("Armstrong numbers within the range " + start + " to " + end + ":");
        printArmstrongNumbers(start, end, p);
    }
}
```

